@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: who owns the question "can this host run a sandbox", and why proveo stopped answering it itself
' covers: internal/sbx/install.go (Prereq, Stage, Prereqs, linuxPrereqs, Blocking, Names, Check, ParseDiagnose, FailedChecks, CheckNames, DiagnoseArgs, DiagnoseAt)
' covers: internal/sbx/install_test.go (TestPrereqsCoverInstallBlockersOnly, TestLinuxPrereqsPassOnAReadyHostAndWarnUnderSELinux, TestParseDiagnoseReadsSbxsOwnVerdict, TestDiagnoseAtAgainstTheRealBinary)
' covers: cmd/proveo/init.go (verify, reportHostReadiness)
' covers: cmd/proveo/init_tui.go (defaultDecisions)
' covers: e2e/init_test.go (TestInitReportsThisHostsPrerequisites, TestInitReadsSbxDiagnoseOnThisHost)
'   the install this gates ......... _spec/cmd/proveo/init-sbx-bootstrap.puml
'   the backend being readied ...... _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
'   what one host can carry ........ _spec/minimum_requirements.puml
' The install plan itself — provenance digests, the CDN post-install, the prompt
' and its locked rows — is init-sbx-bootstrap.puml's, and is not repeated here.
' This file is about ONE question: who answers "can this host run a sandbox".
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE DEFECT: A CHECK THAT WAS A PROXY FOR ANOTHER CHECK ─────────────────
' proveo checked the KVM prerequisite twice, in two different vocabularies.
'
'   1. `sbx.Available()` stats /dev/kvm. Pre-existing, gates every run, and it
'      asks the actual question: is the device there.
'   2. `Prereqs` grew a `kvm` GROUP-MEMBERSHIP check, marked BlocksRun.
'
' The second is a PROXY for "can this user open /dev/kvm", and a proxy is only
' as good as the world it assumes. It is wrong for root, which needs no group.
' It is wrong where udev grants an ACL to the logged-in seat. It is wrong on
' hosts where the group is named `libvirt` or `qemu`. It is wrong for a 0666
' device. Four ways to be in the false-negative direction, which is the
' expensive direction: because the check BLOCKED the run, `proveo init` exited
' non-zero on hosts that work, and the CDN installer — which catches that exit
' and explains it — then told those operators "The sbx backend is not ready
' yet". A correct install, reported as a broken host, by a check proveo had no
' business making.
'
' ── WHO OWNS THE GRANT: NOBODY IN THE PACKAGE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ──────
' Researched 2026-09-07, against the artifacts rather than the folklore:
'
'   the .deb's own postinst   read out of control.tar.xz: `dh_apparmor`
'                             boilerplate ONLY. No kvm, no `usermod`, no group
'                             creation. Its `Depends:` is ca-certificates,
'                             e2fsprogs, dbus-daemon, gnome-keyring — so the
'                             packagers DO declare the prerequisites they can,
'                             and kvm is not one of them.
'   the tarball's install.sh  zero occurrences of kvm, usermod or group.
'   Docker's own docs         `sudo usermod -aG kvm $USER` is written as an
'                             OPERATOR step.
'   the community             docker/sbx-releases#336 answers a failed sandbox
'                             by hand: `usermod -aG kvm $USER` + `newgrp kvm`.
'   and it is not sufficient  docker/sbx-releases#444: the reporter was ALREADY
'                             in `kvm`, KVM worked, and the sandbox still
'                             failed — the missing group was `disk`, for
'                             `/dev/loop-control: permission denied`.
'
'   https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/
'   https://github.com/docker/sbx-releases/issues/336
'   https://github.com/docker/sbx-releases/issues/444
'   https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/linux-postinstall/
'
' Why a postinst legitimately CANNOT do this: it runs as root and does not know
' which human will use the tool, and adding a user to `kvm` is a privilege
' decision, not a packaging step. Docker applies the same pattern to its own
' `docker` group — documented as a manual step and warned as granting
' "root-level privileges". The #444 case is the second half of the argument:
' even a correct grant is not the whole prerequisite, so a tool that hard-codes
' one group is asserting a model of the host it cannot keep true.
'
' ── BUT SBX OWNS THE CHECKING, AND DOES IT BETTER ──────────────────────────
' Declining to REPAIR the host is not the same as declining to REPORT on it.
' `sbx diagnose` has a native Platform › Virtualization check, and measured
' against the real v0.42.0 binary it returns TWELVE rows:
'
'   CLI binary · Binary version · Daemon · Daemon diagnostics · Virtualization
'   Storage directories · Directory permissions · Disk space · Version match
'   Socket · SSH client config · Authentication
'
' Several of those proveo never had at all. Its virtualisation hint names
' nested virtualisation and VDI; proveo's paraphrase never did, and could only
' have gone stale. `--json` yields `checks[]` of {name, status: pass|fail|skip,
' detail, hint} — the same shape as proveo's own `Prereq`, so rendering sbx's
' verdict costs a parse and no translation.
'
' `sbx setup` is NOT the home for this and was checked: its help promises to
' "prepare Docker Sandboxes", but it only imports agent secrets and configures
' SSH forwarding, and it is marked EXPERIMENTAL. diagnose is the verdict.
'
' ── THE RESULTING DIVISION, WHICH IS THIS FILE'S WHOLE POINT ───────────────
' Stage now reads as an OWNERSHIP boundary and not merely a severity:
'
'   BlocksInstall  PROVEO'S. Conditions that decide whether downloading ~100 MB
'                  is worth it, and that must be known BEFORE sbx exists to be
'                  asked: the platform having no asset at all, and e2fsprogs —
'                  the release's install.sh checks for `mkfs.ext4` and exits 2
'                  before copying a single file.
'   BlocksRun      SBX'S. Asked via `DiagnoseAt` AFTER the install, rendered
'                  verbatim with sbx's own hint. proveo reports and instructs;
'                  it never repairs the host. `Prereqs` now yields NOTHING at
'                  this stage on any platform, and a test asserts it.
'   BlocksNothing  PROVEO'S, where sbx is silent. The SELinux warning survives
'                  precisely because `sbx diagnose` has no SELinux check, and
'                  because measured 2026-09-07 the rockylinux8 package did not
'                  resolve an enforcing host either.
'
' Two properties of the asking:
'
' A NON-ZERO EXIT FROM DIAGNOSE IS EXPECTED, not exceptional — it is what sbx
' does whenever any check fails, which is exactly the case proveo is asking
' about. So `DiagnoseAt` parses the payload REGARDLESS of the exit status and
' surfaces the process error only when there is nothing to parse. Treating the
' exit code as the verdict would discard the twelve rows that ARE the verdict.
'
' AND WHEN DIAGNOSE CANNOT BE REACHED AT ALL, proveo says so and points at
' `proveo init --force`. It does not guess, and it does not fall back to the
' proxy checks it just withdrew: "I could not ask" and "the host is fine" are
' different answers, and an empty `checks[]` is an error rather than a clean
' bill of health for the same reason.
'
' ── THE INSTALL POLICY THIS PAIRS WITH ─────────────────────────────────────
' The delegation only makes sense alongside a matching restraint about the
' binary itself: proveo FRESH-INSTALLS sbx, and does nothing else.
'
' An sbx already on the host is the operator's, whatever its version —
' replacing it unasked would be managing their toolchain, which is the same
' overreach as repairing their group memberships. An installed sbx older than
' `MinVersion` is REPORTED with instructions (a run will refuse it anyway)
' rather than silently upgraded. `--force` is the explicit ask, and it is the
' only thing that turns the default from "keep what is installed" back into an
' install.
title host readiness — sbx answers "can this host run a sandbox";\nproveo asks, renders, and installs

actor "operator" as OP

node "the host" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "proveo init — verify\nreportHostReadiness" as INIT <<app>>
  component "internal/sbx.Prereqs\nBlocksInstall + BlocksNothing only" as PRE <<app>>
  component "<prefix>/bin/sbx diagnose --json\n12 checks, each with its own hint" as DIAG <<cloud>>
  database "an sbx already installed\nthe operator's, whatever its version" as OWNED <<db>>
}

cloud "docker-sbx packaging, read 2026-09-07" as PKG <<cloud>> {
  database "postinst: dh_apparmor boilerplate\nDepends: ca-certificates, e2fsprogs,\ndbus-daemon, gnome-keyring" as POST <<db>>
}

component "kvm group membership\nthe proxy check, withdrawn" as PROXY <<error>>
component "SELinux enforcing\nno diagnose row covers it" as SEL <<error>>

OP ARROW_MAIN INIT : ""proveo init""
INIT ARROW_MAIN PRE : before the download — is 100 MB worth it
PRE ARROW_ERROR INIT : BlocksInstall refuses · BlocksNothing warns
INIT ARROW_QUEUE DIAG : after the install, by ABSOLUTE path
DIAG ARROW_MAIN INIT : ""checks[]"" of {name, status, detail, hint}
INIT ARROW_ERROR OP : sbx's own remediation, rendered verbatim
POST ARROW_ERROR PROXY : nothing in the package grants kvm
PROXY ARROW_ERROR INIT : withdrawn — it failed hosts that work
SEL ARROW_MAIN PRE : the one row sbx does not carry
OWNED ARROW_ERROR INIT : left alone unless ""--force""

note right of INIT
  **proveo asks; it never repairs the host.**
  Every failing row is printed with sbx's OWN
  hint. The paraphrase proveo used to print
  named neither nested virtualisation nor VDI,
  and could only ever go stale.
end note

note bottom of PROXY
  **A proxy for a question it cannot answer.**
  "In the kvm group" is wrong for root, for a
  udev ACL grant, for ""libvirt"" or ""qemu"", and
  for a 0666 device. Marked BlocksRun, it made
  ""proveo init"" exit non-zero on working hosts.
end note

note bottom of POST
  **The packagers grant no privilege either.**
  The .deb postinst is ""dh_apparmor"" and
  nothing else: no kvm, no ""usermod"", no group.
  It declares what it can — ca-certificates,
  e2fsprogs, dbus-daemon, gnome-keyring.
end note

note bottom of PKG
  **The grant is an operator step, by design.**
  A postinst runs as root and cannot know which
  human will use the tool. Docker treats its own
  ""docker"" group the same way, warned as
  granting "root-level privileges".
end note

note left of DIAG
  **Twelve rows, several proveo never had.**
  Measured on v0.42.0: CLI binary, version,
  daemon, daemon diagnostics, virtualization,
  storage dirs, permissions, disk, version
  match, socket, SSH config, authentication.
end note

note bottom of DIAG
  **A non-zero exit is the NORMAL case.** sbx
  fails whenever a check fails, so DiagnoseAt
  parses the payload regardless and reports a
  process error only with nothing to parse. If
  it cannot be asked: say so, name ""--force"".
end note

note bottom of PRE
  **BlocksInstall is the half proveo keeps.**
  Both of them must be knowable BEFORE sbx is
  here to be asked: no asset for this platform,
  and ""mkfs.ext4"" — the release's install.sh
  exits 2 before it copies a single file.
end note

note bottom of SEL
  **Kept only because sbx is silent here.**
  ""sbx diagnose"" carries no SELinux row, and
  measured 2026-09-07 the rockylinux8 package
  did not resolve an enforcing host either. So
  BlocksNothing: worth saying, blocks nobody.
end note

note bottom of OWNED
  **Fresh installs only; the rest is theirs.**
  Replacing an sbx nobody asked about is the
  same overreach as fixing someone's groups.
  Older than MinVersion is REPORTED — a run
  refuses it anyway — never quietly upgraded.
end note
@enduml
